---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ProbabilityCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc2/ProbabilityCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="probability-calculator">
  <ProbabilityCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the probability calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Enter the chance of event A and of event B, as percentages or decimals. The probability calculator
      treats them as independent, so one does not change the chance of the other, and works out the chance of
      every combination:
    </p>
    <pre><code>P(A and B)  = P(A) × P(B)
P(A or B)   = P(A) + P(B) − P(A) × P(B)
P(not A)    = 1 − P(A)
P(exactly one) = P(A) + P(B) − 2 P(A) P(B)
P(neither)  = (1 − P(A)) × (1 − P(B))
P(A at least once in n trials) = 1 − (1 − P(A))ⁿ</code></pre>
    <p>
      The square shows the same sums as areas. A takes a strip on the left as wide as P(A), and B a band at
      the top as tall as P(B). Where they overlap is P(A and B), because the area of a rectangle is width
      times height.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Rain is forecast at 50% and your train is late 40% of the time, and the two have nothing to do with
      each other. The chance of both is 0.5 × 0.4 = 0.2, or 20%. The chance of at least one is 0.5 + 0.4 −
      0.2 = 0.7, or 70%. The chance of neither is 0.5 × 0.6 = 0.3, or 30%.
    </p>
    <p>
      At least once: a six on a die has P = 1/6. In four rolls, the chance of no six is (5/6)⁴ = 0.482, so
      the chance of at least one six is 1 − 0.482 = 0.518, or 51.8%. The simulation under the square throws
      such runs again and again, and the share of runs with at least one success settles near the formula.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The events must be independent. For dependent events, such as two cards drawn from one deck, P(A and
        B) = P(A) × P(B given A), which needs the conditional probability.
      </li>
      <li>
        Events that cannot happen together (mutually exclusive) are not independent: for them P(A and B) = 0
        and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).
      </li>
      <li>The simulation uses the browser's random number generator and shows the idea; the numbers come from the formulas.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
